Positional encoding is a technique used in Transformer models to capture the inference of word order and position within a sequence for sequences of arbitrary length. Transformers, being a type of self-attention model, have difficulty understanding the position of words in a sequence because they do not have an inherent notion of order. Positional encoding is used to feed this information into the model, allowing it to better capture the underlying structure of the sequence.

The positional encoding is added to the input sequence embeddings before they are passed through the transformer encoder layers. It is typically implemented as a sine and cosine function of the position in the sequence, providing a continuous representation of position that can be easily summed with the word embeddings.
[6/200] Running: How does self-attention differ from cross-attention?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 In the context of transformer models, self-attention is used to measure the significance of words in a single sentence or sequence. It computes the attention scores between each word in the sequence and every other word in the same sequence. On the other hand, cross-attention, also known as encoder-decoder attention, is used in the case of an encoder-decoder model, where the encoder provides input for the decoder. In cross-attention, the decoder attends to the encoder input words, and the encoder attends to the decoder's hidden states, allowing them to exchange information.
[7/200] Running: Explain the concept of tokenization in NLP....

