Data Parallelism:
Data Parallelism is a type of parallelism where the same operation is performed on different data simultaneously. It is a technique to exploit the data-level parallelism which capability exists in modern multi-core processors.

For example, consider a matrix multiplication operation. If we have two matrices A(m x n) and B(n x p), the result C(m x p) can be obtained by multiplying each element in A with the corresponding elements in B and summing the products for all the n values to get the element in C.

In data parallelism, each core can take one row of matrix A and perform the multiplication and sum operation on all the columns of matrix B for that specific row simultaneously.

Pipeline Parallelism:
Pipeline Parallelism is a technique that breaks down the operations of a single task into several stages. The output of one stage becomes the input for the next stage, similar to a real-life assembly line. This technique helps to hide the latency of slow stages by overlapping the computation of different stages.

For example, in a pipeline for adding two numbers, we have four stages: Move Operands, Add Ends, Add Middles, and Add Begins. The first stage moves the operands into the pipeline. The second stage adds the ends of the operands, the third stage adds the middle parts, and the fourth stage combines the result from the third stage with the initial values to produce the final sum. By the time the second stage is performing its operation, the first stage can already be loaded with new operands, thus hiding the latency of the slow stages.

In the context of GPU computing, data parallelism is often used to perform the same operation on different data, while pipeline parallelism is used to hide the latency of slow operations within a single CPU or GPU core. Both techniques are useful in achieving high performance for parallel computations.
