ALiBi positional encoding is designed to efficiently capture both local and global dependencies in the data, improving the performance of models like Transformers. Unlike other positional encoding methods, ALiBi is noise-contrastive, meaning that it learns to distinguish between valid and invalid positions in the sequence. This helps the model to generalize better and to avoid errors caused by overfitting.

In ALiBi, the positional encoding is learned by minimizing a contrastive loss function. The model is trained to predict whether a processed position embedding vector corresponds to the correct position in the sequence or a randomly perturbed position. This process allows the model to learn a meaningful and differentiable representation of each position in the sequence.

The ALiBi positional encoding method has been shown to provide significant improvements in the performance of Transformer-based models on a variety of tasks, especially for long sequences, which can be challenging for models that rely on positional encodings.
