## BERT and Multilingual Text: What Happens Behind the Scenes

To process multilingual text with BERT, we first need a multilingual BERT (mBERT) model, which has been trained on a diverse corpus of text from over 100 languages. During pre-training, each token is represented by its own embedding, which captures the semantic, linguistic, and character-level information of a given token across all languages. The embeddings are trained to capture similar contextual information for the same words across different languages, encouraging the model to learn universal patterns.

Once the mBERT model is trained, it can be fine-tuned on specific language tasks like language modeling, text classification, named entity recognition, etc. Fine-tuning involves using a smaller dataset related to the specific task to further adapt and specialize the mBERT model to the given language and task.

